---
// Project page anatomy per the dh-site-publish skill: identity, research question, the
// interactive object high on the page, evidence, findings, limits, data and citation.
// This project is an investigation: the finished work is the source audit, not a framing analysis.
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import ProjectNav from '../../../components/ProjectNav.astro';
import ResearchQuestion from '../../../components/ResearchQuestion.astro';
import ProjectFacts from '../../../components/ProjectFacts.astro';
import Apparatus from '../../../components/Apparatus.astro';
import HallyuCoverage from '../../../components/figures/HallyuCoverage.astro';
import HallyuSeries from '../../../components/figures/HallyuSeries.astro';
import hallyu from '../../../data/hallyu.json';
import { projects } from '../../../data/projects';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'hallyu-indian-press')!;
const S = hallyu.print_series;
const blocks = hallyu.periodisation.blocks;
const trough = S.rows.find((r) => r.year === 2017)!;

const scale = [
  { n: hallyu.korea_baseline_2000_2010.toLocaleString('en-US'), unit: 'Korea-related articles, 2000–2010' },
  { n: String(hallyu.hallyu_term_uses_2000_2010), unit: 'use of “Hallyu” in that decade' },
  { n: S.total.toLocaleString('en-US'), unit: 'print articles, 2006–2026' },
  { n: '6', unit: 'archives audited' },
  { n: String(hallyu.pilot.corpus_docs), unit: 'documents in the pilot corpus' },
];

const sections = [
  { id: 'question', label: 'Question' },
  { id: 'explore', label: 'Explore' },
  { id: 'evidence', label: 'Evidence' },
  { id: 'findings', label: 'Findings' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits & rights' },
  { id: 'data', label: 'Data & citation' },
];

const jsonld = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press, 2000–2026: source audit and counts',
  description:
    'Counts and source-coverage metadata for Indian English-language newspaper reporting on Korean popular culture, 2000–2026. Newspaper text is copyrighted and is not redistributed; the dataset publishes counts, coverage windows and derived statistics only.',
  creator: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Mohd Bilal', affiliation: { '@type': 'CollegeOrUniversity', name: 'The Academy of Korean Studies' } },
  temporalCoverage: '2000/2026',
  spatialCoverage: 'India',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
  version: 'Source audit v1, 18 August 2026',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  variableMeasured: [
    'Korea-related article count', 'Hallyu-vocabulary article count',
    'licensed full-text coverage start year', 'print versus online edition objects',
  ],
};
---
<Project title="Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press"
  description="A source audit and corpus-development project on Indian English-language newspaper reporting of Korean popular culture, 2000–2026. The national press was nearly silent on Hallyu before 2011, and the visibility curve turns in 2018–19."
  citationTitle="Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press, 2000–2026: A Source Audit"
  jsonld={jsonld}>

  <div class="wrap">
    <ProjectHeader project={p} scale={scale} />
  </div>

  <ProjectNav sections={sections} />

  <div class="wrap">
    <ResearchQuestion status="Status: the source audit is complete and is reported here as a result rather than as preparation. The framing analysis it was built to make possible has not begun, and nothing on this page is a claim about how the press framed anything.">
      <p>
        What can Indian English-language newspaper archives actually support as evidence about
        Korean popular culture, and where do they fail?
      </p>
    </ResearchQuestion>

    <section class="section flush" id="explore" aria-labelledby="explore-h">
      <h2 id="explore-h"><span class="mark">Explore</span>Twenty years of print coverage, paper by paper</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Every print-edition article matching the project's candidate Hallyu vocabulary, by year and
          by newspaper. Isolate a paper to see its own share of each year: the swing between them is
          why provenance is a core variable of this design rather than a nuisance field.
        </p>
      </div>
      <HallyuSeries />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The periodisation under the bars is derived from the series rather than assumed, which is
          the methodological point of the whole exercise. An earlier working hypothesis put the break
          at 2017, on the strength of a global BTS breakout; the evidence makes 2017 a local trough
          at {trough.total} articles, 0.61 times the previous year, with the step change arriving in
          two stages afterwards.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <caption class="small muted">Mean print articles per year, by block. The final partial year is excluded from every rate.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Block</th><th scope="col" class="num">Articles per year</th><th scope="col">Character</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {blocks.map((b) => (
              <tr><td class="mono">{b.span}</td><td class="num">{b.per_year}</td><td>{b.character}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <Apparatus label="Which paper carries the story">
        <p>
          The Hindustan Times share of print coverage swings widely and is worth explaining rather
          than smoothing: 80% in 2006 on very small numbers, down to 13.6% in 2013, and back to 80.3%
          in 2024. It is both the earliest substantial carrier and the dominant one in the saturation
          period. A wire story is not evidence of a newspaper's framing choices, which is why staff,
          wire and unknown provenance is recorded per document.
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-h">
      <h2 id="evidence-h"><span class="mark">Evidence</span>An absence, and the archives that change underneath it</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          During the decade in which Korean popular culture was spreading through Northeast India,
          the English-language national press was nearly silent about it. Licensed full-text search
          across the Times of India, Hindustan Times and Indian Express finds
          {hallyu.korea_baseline_2000_2010.toLocaleString('en-US')} Korea-related articles in
          2000–2010, and the words “Hallyu” or “Korean wave” appear exactly
          {hallyu.hallyu_term_uses_2000_2010} time in the whole period: a single incidental wire-story
          use in 2007. Only 58 articles match the project's broader candidate vocabulary before 2011,
          almost all in film-review contexts.
        </p>
        <p>
          A parallel open-web harvest of The Hindu's daily section indexes for the same decade,
          enumerated through the Internet Archive, recovered
          {hallyu.pilot.hindu_headline_matches} Korea-related headlines and a
          {hallyu.pilot.corpus_docs}-document pilot corpus, and zero culture-context Hallyu
          headlines. Two independent routes into the same decade return the same absence.
        </p>
        <p>
          The silence is national, not total. Korean popular culture was circulating regionally
          through these years, and the project records that circulation in a lightweight evidence
          register rather than treating it as a corpus, because the material behind it is not
          comparable with national newspaper text and would not survive being counted alongside it.
          Keeping the two apart is what allows the absence in the national press to be read as a fact
          about the national press.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Apparatus label="Why this is a finding and not a gap">
        <p>
          The sources were searched, the criteria are stated, and the retrieval was validated against
          licensed full text. A documented near-zero under stated criteria is evidence about what the
          press was doing. It is distinct from the 2000–2004 window below, where no licensed source
          exists at all and the correct description is unvalidated.
        </p>
      </Apparatus>

      <div class="subsection">
        <h3>The archives change underneath the curve</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Counts of Korean-culture articles do rise steeply after 2011, and they cannot be read as
            popularity until the thing being counted holds still. Each database begins its full text
            in a different year, between 2005 and 2015, and from 2017 all of them start indexing
            online-only editions alongside print. A count that mixes those objects will rise whether
            or not any newspaper published more.
          </p>
        </div>
        <HallyuCoverage />
        <Apparatus label="A rule this project holds itself to">
          <p>
            The phrase “Hallyu coverage increased” is not used in this project, and variables are
            named so that Korean-popular-culture counts and Hallyu-vocabulary counts can never be
            silently conflated. Print objects are separated from online objects before any
            comparison, and counts are reported per newspaper per year rather than as a single
            national curve.
          </p>
        </Apparatus>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="findings" aria-labelledby="findings-h">
      <h2 id="findings-h"><span class="mark">Findings</span>What the audit establishes</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ol class="findlist">
          <li>
            <b>The national press was nearly silent on Hallyu through the decade it was arriving.</b>
            Ten thousand Korea-related articles, one use of the word, and an independent open-web
            harvest that returns the same zero. That is a documented absence under stated criteria,
            not a gap in the search, and it contradicts the assumption that press attention tracks
            cultural presence. <a class="backto" href="#evidence">See the evidence →</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>The visibility curve cannot be read until the denominator is held constant,</b> and
            once it is, it turns in 2018–19 rather than 2017. A periodisation taken from the history
            of the phenomenon would have put the boundary in the wrong place, and one taken from the
            raw database counts would have put it in the same wrong place for a different reason.
            <a class="backto" href="#series">See the curve →</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>The constraints are themselves a publishable result.</b> The GDELT floor at 2017, the
            per-paper full-text start years, the online-edition break and the web-archive
            preservation curve are undocumented in the databases' own materials and apply to anyone
            building a corpus from the Indian English-language press.
            <a class="backto" href="#coverage">See the coverage windows →</a>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="method">
      <h2><span class="mark">Method</span>How the corpus is being built</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A three-stage pipeline. <b>Discover</b> enumerates archive discovery pages.
          <b>Harvest</b> fetches candidates cache-first at polite, identified, rate-limited speeds,
          with every HTTP response cached permanently so that re-running a stage never re-touches the
          network. <b>Build</b> compiles a full-text-searchable corpus in SQLite with FTS5, carrying
          provenance for every document.
        </p>
        <p>
          Vocabulary matching is deliberately two-tier: a broad Korea net for recall, then the
          candidate Hallyu vocabulary for precision, evaluated against licensed full-text counts
          rather than assumed. The vocabulary is version 0.9 and is not frozen: it will be fixed only
          after a retrieval test measures its recall and precision on a stratified sample. No paywall
          and no robots directive is bypassed at any stage; where an archive is bot-gated, the
          licensed copy is the only route used.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
      <h2 id="limits-h"><span class="mark">Limits and rights</span>What will be published, and what never will</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Newspaper text is copyrighted, and this project will not republish it. That is a constraint
          on the shape of the output rather than on its substance: what makes the corpus useful to
          another researcher is the description of it, not the articles themselves.
        </p>
        <ul class="willpublish">
          <li><b>Counts by newspaper and year,</b> print objects separated from online objects, as a citable table with a deposited version.</li>
          <li><b>The source-coverage audit:</b> database windows, the GDELT floor, and web-archive capture rates by year.</li>
          <li><b>The candidate Hallyu vocabulary</b> (v0.9) and the relevance decision tree with its boundary rulings.</li>
          <li><b>Provenance shares</b> — staff, wire, unknown — per newspaper per period.</li>
          <li><b>No article text, ever,</b> and no derived text long enough to substitute for it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>And what the project does not claim:</p>
        <ul class="limits">
          <li><b>No framing analysis exists yet.</b> Everything here is a count or a statement about source availability. No frame, topic or evaluation coding has been applied to a validated sample.</li>
          <li><b>2000–2004 is unvalidated.</b> No licensed full-text source at the host institution covers it, and web-archive coverage of that window is thinnest.</li>
          <li><b>The pre-2011 absence still awaits recall validation</b> against a historical database before it can be published as a strong claim.</li>
          <li><b>Discovery in the web-archive strand is headline-level,</b> which is a known recall limitation.</li>
          <li><b>One annual figure is treated as suspect:</b> the Times of India count for {S.anomaly.year} falls to {S.anomaly.value} between neighbours of 207 and 128, which is more likely a database gap than an editorial decision.</li>
          <li><b>The final year is partial</b> ({S.partial_note}) and is never used in a rate.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small muted">
          The full source audit, pipeline description, figure grammar and citation are on the
          <a href="/projects/hallyu-indian-press/data/">data page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="data" aria-labelledby="facts-h">
      <ProjectFacts
        project={p}
        duration="Source feasibility work from August 2026; pilot corpus and licensed validation completed 18 August 2026; collection phases gated and in progress."
        resources={[
          { href: '/projects/hallyu-indian-press/data/', label: 'Method and data', note: 'sources, pipeline, ethics, figure grammar' },
          { href: '#series', label: 'Print counts by paper and year', note: 'the figure’s data table, 2006–2026' },
        ]}
        licence="Counts, coverage metadata and derived statistics: CC BY 4.0. Pipeline code: MIT. Newspaper text is copyrighted, is held locally under licence, and is not redistributed in any form."
        citation="Bilal, Mohd. Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press, 2000–2026: A Source Audit. Source audit v1. The Academy of Korean Studies, 2026."
        url="https://mohdbilaldh.github.io/projects/hallyu-indian-press/"
        credits="Research, data, code and design: Mohd Bilal (The Academy of Korean Studies). Licensed database access through Yonsei University. No external funding." />
    </section>
  </div>
</Project>
<style>
  .flush { margin-top: 0; }
  .subsection { margin-top: var(--s-5); }
  .limits, .willpublish, .findlist { max-width: var(--w-measure); }
  .limits li, .willpublish li, .findlist li { margin-bottom: 12px; }
</style>
